  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "Analysis combining high-resolution asset wealth data with hourly temperature records across Africa from 2000 to 2023 demonstrates that extreme heat reduced annual continental asset wealth growth by 2.42%. Rural communities lost 2.54% of annual growth compared to 0.34% in urban centers, with damage escalating further from urban cores. The poorest settlements forfeited nearly 50% of their annual asset growth due to extreme heat, whereas the wealthiest settlements lost 1.24%, causing Middle Africa to lose 16.33% of its annual growth.",
      "why": "This quantifies how extreme climate events actively widen economic inequality across a continent by selectively stripping growth from remote, low-wealth populations while leaving wealthier urban centers relatively insulated."
    }
  },
